      ref: "Matthew 6:7; Matthew 6:8; Ecclesiastes 5:2; Proverbs 10:19; Proverbs 17:27; Proverbs 17:28; Proverbs 11:1; Proverbs 16:11; Proverbs 20:10; Leviticus 19:35; Leviticus 19:36; Matthew 5:37; James 5:12; Ecclesiastes 10:10; Proverbs 24:27; 1 Corinthians 14:40; Luke 14:28; Proverbs 21:5; 1 Kings 3:9; Hebrews 5:14; Isaiah 1:18; Proverbs 18:13; Habakkuk 2:2; Proverbs 27:23; Deuteronomy 19:15; 2 Corinthians 13:1; 1 Thessalonians 5:21; Luke 16:10; Luke 16:11; 1 Corinthians 4:2; Job 28:12; Proverbs 25:2; Psalms 127:1",
      theme: "\"A false balance is abomination to the LORD: but a just weight is his delight.\" (Proverbs 11:1) The weight that reads the same every time is not a technical preference; it is His delight. \"If the iron be blunt, and he do not whet the edge, then must he put to more strength: but wisdom is profitable to direct.\" (Ecclesiastes 10:10) Paying more strength for the same dull edge is the exact waste the headline names. \"Give therefore thy servant an understanding heart to judge thy people, that I may discern between good and bad\" (1 Kings 3:9) Judgment is a thing to be asked for and exercised, not avoided - and not spent on arithmetic. \"Write the vision, and make it plain upon tables, that he may run that readeth it.\" (Habakkuk 2:2) A result nobody can read back is not finished, however good it looked.",
