    bigIdea: "ORDER CARRIES MEANING — THE SAME WORDS REARRANGED SAY ANOTHER THING, AND THAT IS HOW A LIE IS MADE. Darrell forwarded a ByteByteGo article on 2026-10-13 with one word on it: Lesson. This source is an honest one — it prints its own disclaimer, lists nine references, and states its own limits, so we take it plainly rather than hedging a true thing into opinion. Its example is three words long: Alice trusts Bob, and Bob trusts Alice, identical words asserting opposite things. And the surprising part is that the words already arrive in order and that is still not enough, because an array's layout does not become an input feature until the work actually uses it — shuffle a list of numbers and the sum is unchanged. Scripture settled order first, and as instruction: \"Let all things be done decently and in order.\" (1 Corinthians 14:40), assigned rather than emergent (\"But now hath God set the members every one of them in the body, as it hath pleased him.\" (1 Corinthians 12:18)), and enumerated where it must be unmistakable — first apostles, secondarily prophets, thirdly teachers (1 Corinthians 12:28). Then the heart of it: when the adversary tempted Jesus he quoted the Psalms accurately and cut the clause \"to keep thee in all thy ways\" out of the middle, splicing the next verse over the gap, and that rearrangement alone turned a promise of Yahweh's keeping into an invitation to jump. He invented nothing; he moved something. Jesus answers not with doubt and not with an argument of His own, but with more of the Word: \"Jesus said unto him, It is written again, Thou shalt not tempt the Lord thy God.\" (Matthew 4:7)",
    inApp: "This week in the app, open this lesson under Sovereign A.I. and read it at your band, then read the same passage at a second band and notice what the order of the teaching does. Then take one quotation somebody handed you recently and go read what sits immediately before it and immediately after it in its own source. Write down whether the surrounding words change what it means.",
