      "research": "Take one quotation that was handed to you in the last month — a verse used in a sermon or an argument, a statistic in a headline, or a sentence somebody attributed to another person. Find its actual source and read the sentence immediately before it and the sentence immediately after it. Write both down next to the quotation. Do not reason about whether it was fair; write the surrounding words out and let them speak.",
      "plan": "Now compare. Does the surrounding material change what the quotation means, narrow it, qualify it, or reverse it? Matthew 4:6 is the pattern to measure against: the words were genuine and a limiting clause was removed, so look specifically for what is MISSING rather than for what is wrong. Then decide what you owe: nothing, a correction to yourself, or a word to whoever passed it to you. If the quotation was Scripture, find what is written again on the same subject and write that reference down beside it.",
      "execute": "Do one thing with it this week, and make it the smaller, harder one. If you passed the quotation along yourself, go back to the person you gave it to and give them the surrounding words too. If somebody handed it to you, answer the way Jesus answered — not by calling the words false, but by bringing more of the Word, or more of the source. Then, in whatever you are teaching, writing or building next, put the order of it in writing before you start, because the arrangement is part of what you are saying."
