    "title": "The agent that drowned in its own output - a bigger window is not a sounder mind, the pointer that keeps what matters, and the clear \"It is finished\"",
    "bigIdea": "A newsletter Darrell marked \"Lesson\" reported a measured failure that is a parable of the mind. An agent watching an application's logs was handed 214KB of log text by one tool; its answers got worse, and nothing threw an error. Re-run in an open-source agent framework, eight log fetches left the agent holding about 252,000 tokens of history. One plugin cut that to about 9,000 - not by reading less, but by putting the pile in storage and handing the agent a short pointer it could fetch by. The cheap shortcut, a smaller window, cut it further and was worse: it threw away whole turns, including whatever the agent had learned in them. And an agent that called the same tool fourteen times dropped to two once it was given a clear \"done\" signal. The Word settled every part of this before there were tokens to count. The mind is not kept by holding everything at once but by taking thoughts captive: \"Casting down imaginations, and every high thing that exalteth itself against the knowledge of God, and bringing into captivity every thought to the obedience of Christ;\" (2 Corinthians 10:5) More is not the remedy - \"For in much wisdom is much grief: and he that increaseth knowledge increaseth sorrow.\" (Ecclesiastes 1:18) The pile goes outside and a sign stays behind, which is what Yahweh told Joshua to do with twelve stones: \"Then ye shall answer them, That the waters of Jordan were cut off before the ark of the covenant of the LORD; when it passed over Jordan, the waters of Jordan were cut off: and these stones shall be for a memorial unto the children of Israel for ever.\" (Joshua 4:7) Forgetting what you learned is the cheap window, and it is the sin of a generation: \"They soon forgat his works; they waited not for his counsel:\" (Psalms 106:13) And the labour ends because the work is finished, not because someone gave up: \"When Jesus therefore had received the vinegar, he said, It is finished: and he bowed his head, and gave up the ghost.\" (John 19:30) This week teaches the sound mind the Word describes, in the machine and in the man, and names plainly what this house did not check.",
    "inApp": "Open the app and read one of your own Living Lessons end to end without scrolling back: notice that the lesson hands you an anchor reference, not the whole Bible, and that Search it out fetches the text live from the family's own corpus when you ask for it - that is a pointer, not a pile. Then open Admin -> Systems and read Loop Health: the live numbers come from real runs, and each row says when it was last measured rather than holding every run in view. THEN in your Study, write the one thing you keep re-deciding, and the sentence that would make it decided once.",
